action with the  communities and you address it  immediately it's it's instant doing that  you see an immediate reduction in crime  and it's not just the crime get  it goes elsewhere it actually seems to  act to like minimize the amount of crime  what you're staying on top of it you're  staying on top of it and it's active its  real-time enforcement versus after the  fact following trends what what do you  think explains in place cities like San  Diego for instance had a steeper decline  in crime than New York it did not use  kind of the community policing or the  concepts of what do you think was going  on in the 90s because through about the  middle point of the 90s all the criminal  just was predicting the rise of super  predators etc and then pretty much  everywhere across the country crime  rates just collapsed well you can't say  I mean New York City I have a pretty  good idea of what we did because I was  there and what we did with regard to  crime reduction in the City Police  Department and in the city of New York I  did on Rikers Island this same kind of  oversight let's talk a little bit about  that because you as the head of  Corrections you ever saw a place like  Rikers Island which is you know infamous  for violence among inmates it was when  you oversaw a stark steep decline in the  amount of the criminal and criminal or  an inmate on inmate five riots what how  did you do that one the the same exact  method that the police department  honoring crime I started looking at  violence in the system and addressing it  daily increasing our searches holding  the inmates accountable for criminal  action you can't in a jail of prison you  cannot have it can't be a haven for  criminal activity and you know to give  you an example one of my first the most  violent incidents I saw it Rikers two  inmates held another inmate down on the  ground and carved lk4 Latin king into  his back with the chicken or a turkey  bone a hundred and eighty stitches when  I asked what happened to the inmate that  assaulted the inmates that assaulted  this kid he was put in special housing  was he charged with a crime no why my  house it's a jail that's what they do no  no you can't go into a prison facility  or a jail facility and attempt to murder  someone or cut them to pieces and think  that that's okay  so I started charging the  mates with violent crimes with  possession of deadly weapons the same  same thing that would be charged with on  the outside I also created an  accountability system to monitor monitor  staff assaults on inmates interaction  with inmates that kind of what happened  you know the New York Times just had a  report come out that particularly under  the last term of bloomer this was long  after you were involved in in New York  PD that actually the guards a lot of  interactions negative interactions with  inmates spiked up what do you think was  going on there here's what happens  whether it's a presidential  administration a state government or a  local government new people come in to  take over they ignore the policies that  worked they create their own and  whatever happens happens the leadership  and the accountability that was  instilled when I turned over the  department in 2000 completely went out  the window and as a result there has  been substantial increases in violence  on Rikers they have 10,000 less inmates  today than I had ten thousand yet their  violence is continually increasing there  were a number of corruption  investigations as a result of internal  things at Rikers the accountability and  leadership was shot and and I think that  was demonstrated in the New York Times  piece and more importantly over a  two-and-a-half year investigation that  the US Attorney's Office conducted and  and put out a report what do you make of  the the current case that's a super hot  of a abuse of power of police and in New  York the chokehold death of Eric garner  you've been critical of al Sharpton and  people like that or criticized the cops  but I mean what does it say that you're  putting a guy who's being charged with  selling loose cigarettes and a chokehold  is you know what has gone wrong with the  system  well here's here's the problem and I  think this is what a lot of people don't  understand whether the cops were  arresting the guy for selling cigarettes  or whether they were arresting him from  mass murder  once you tell someone is under arrest  and he resists you have to use whatever  force necessary to affect that arrest in  this case you have from what I saw in  the video and I think it was three guys  trying to arrest this guy - wait 380  pounds could it have been done  differently that's gonna be a for a  grand jury to decide the chokehold you  know is is something that in fact I  think it was Bill Bratton 20 years ago  that basically said you know you're  going against the policy if you use it  the problem you have is when you're  trying to take down somebody like that  that big that's strong you put them in a  submission hold at some point in time  you cut off their airway and it turns  into a chokehold here's here's my issue  with the whole thing it's people  shouldn't comment until the  investigations done I tried out not to  Monday Morning Quarterback anybody doing  the job that I know it's done because  I've done it my fear in arresting  somebody like a garner somebody that's  bigger than me some of these it could  overpower me is that they do overpower  me and they get my gun that's a fear  every cop has because if they get that  gun that's the best way though I mean  there's clearly among you know in not  just in New York and arguably less in  New York than in other cities there's  real tension particularly along racial  lines of you know black populations feel  like they're being overrun by the police  or they're being you know occupied by  the police what's the best way to you  know kind of deescalate that tension has  to be constant communication 