sive neighborhood that you have to  work i don't know if that's a benefit to  community relations because i'm not  talking to anybody  i'm not saying nothing i'm not gonna  have any interaction with any why why  not because anything you say is gonna be  interpreted by somebody that has no idea  what you're talking about  about what you mean you know if there's  a way to activate a camera when you go  into a combat situation or you go into a  confrontational situation there's a way  to do that maybe but just picture this i  put a camera on you for eight hours a  day and i'm gonna listen to everything  you say for eight hours  it's gonna be a matter of public record  because technically that's what's gonna  happen is that something you would want  does it invade your personal privacy of  course it does talk a little bit about  what it felt like you know your your  career was you know just the kind of  transcendent awkward arc upwards right  up into the moment when you were  nominated to be the the second secretary  of the department of homeland security  after Tom Ridge that's when all of your  problems started what did it feel like  to have you know just that type of world  start crumbling you know underneath your  feet there are no words to describe that  I had a 35 year career that was pretty  much unparalleled I had successes and  achievements in law enforcement that  nobody has matched in the country  especially where Rikers manok and then  to have the same government that I've  fought for and nearly died for more  times that I can count  target you you know initially over my  nanny  you know not paying tax on my my  children's nanny and there was a  undocumented immigrant right from Mexico  and did you know that she was  undocumented yeah do you think that  should be a crime personally yeah no no  I mean you know there are people in this  country really super super hard-working  people  this woman that worked for my wife and I  a loving caring treated my kids like  there were her own you know I'm  horrified that it happened but I loved  her to death and you know a good woman  and there are there are thousands out  there that do this you know I think  there's got to be a way to to fix that  in this immigration stuff regardless  when your entire world is then  scrutinized and and that's what happens  when you become the target of an  investigation like this it when it goes  from the nanny  to my apartment renovations and then to  everything your business your taxes your  anything in everything everything you  can't even imagine  every phone call anything you've ever  done is now scrutinized for the past ten  years it's overwhelming and I mean  obviously they dug up more than enough  to put you in jail do you think that  would happen to anybody I mean to go  back to your where we started you know  talking about clemency are talking about  sentencing reform are we living in a  world of you know one of reasons  contributors AB wrote a book called  three felonies a day which basically  said that everything has been  criminalized to a point where we're all  guilty of something every high school  student in America before they graduate  high school should be forced to read  silver glades book every high school  student because no one in America  understands and knows that if you give  me a stack of subpoenas the size of this  pack and give me the power to scrutinize  you as they did me I promise you you're  going to prison anybody in this room  anybody on that street anybody in  America can go to prison if you give me  subpoenas and give me the power to  investigate anyone all right well we  will leave it there that's a a happy  ending we've been talking with Bernie  Kerik he's the former commissioner of  New York City and he's the principal at  the carrot group now thanks for talking  with us  thank you sir a reason TV I'm Nick  Gillespie
 

